        "number": 17648,
        "title": "BN_mod_exp2_mont NULL pointer dereference if modulus is 0",
        "body": "`BN_mod_exp2_mont` accesses the data field of the modulus without checking if it is allocated:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/77f3936928068bee9d7e0c6939709ac179cb1059/crypto/bn/bn_exp2.c#L35\r\n\r\nSo calling `BN_mod_exp2_mont` with a zero modulus can lead to a NULL pointer dereference.\r\n\r\nInternally, this function is only used by DSA verification:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/77f3936928068bee9d7e0c6939709ac179cb1059/crypto/dsa/dsa_ossl.c#L402\r\n\r\nIt would be possible to crash a TLS client connection with crafted data (specifically by defining a zero `p` value) if it weren't for the fact that `dsa->flags` is by default set to `DSA_FLAG_CACHE_MONT_P`:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/77f3936928068bee9d7e0c6939709ac179cb1059/crypto/dsa/dsa_ossl.c#L428\r\n\r\nThis preloads a Montgomery structure with the modulus: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/77f3936928068bee9d7e0c6939709ac179cb1059/crypto/dsa/dsa_ossl.c#L391\r\n\r\nThis rejects a zero modulus, so in that case `BN_mod_exp2_mont` is never reached, and a crash is avoided.\r\n\r\nApart from causing a NULL pointer dereference, this bug also might be leveraged as a side channel leak of 1 bit if the modulus passed to `BN_mod_exp2_mont` was previously used to store other (secret) data; data in `d` can be retained even if `top` is set to zero, for example after calling `BN_zero()`, and observing whether `BN_mod_exp2_mont` succeeds or fails reveals the least significant bit.\r\n\r\nDespite the bug not being remotely reachable at this moment, I suggest adding a check to `BN_mod_exp2_mont` anyway:\r\n\r\n- Users may be using `BN_mod_exp2_mont` directly\r\n- Users can unset the `DSA_FLAG_CACHE_MONT_P` manually\r\n\r\nFor demonstration purposes, below is an input to the client fuzzer which causes it to crash if you disable `DSA_FLAG_CACHE_MONT_P`.\r\n\r\n```\r\nunsigned char crasher_bin[] = {\r\n  0x16, 0x03, 0x0b, 0x00, 0x3d, 0x02, 0x00, 0x00, 0x39, 