            "review_comments": [
                "This error isn't quite accurate with this change. Just because it's not odd, doesn't make it even.",
                "I'm not sure I understand the comment but this still has the NULL dereference.\r\n\r\nBN_is_odd() handles zero length BN's fine but not NULL pointers.  It should.\r\nThe question then becomes is NULL odd or even?",
                "The original bug was for where m is non-NULL but `m->d` is NULL.\r\n\r\nIt's plausible we should handle NULL m safely but we currently don't handle NULL a1, p1, a2, p2 or rr safely either.\r\n\r\nExamining the BN code more generally, it doesn't seem like it's the done thing to error or assert on preconditions like \"m is not NULL\". In other words, it seems to be the convention in BN that unless otherwise specified, `BIGNUM*` arguments are mandatory and unchecked, with it being the callers responsibility to ensure the argument is non-NULL. Am I mistaken here? `bn_check_top` is commonly used but this only comes into play in debug builds.",
                "Yes, there is no need to check for m == NULL.",
